The reduce() function in JS is a powerful method for transforming an array into a single value by iteratively applying
a callback function. It can be used to accumulate values, perform calculations, or even transform arrays into other
datastructures like objects

array.reduce((accumulator, currentValue, currentIndex, array) => {
  // Return the updated accumulator
}, initialValue);

accumulator: The accumulated result from the previous iteration (or the initialValue on the first iteration).
currentValue: The current element being processed in the array.
currentIndex: The index of the currentValue being processed (optional).
array: The original array reduce() was called on (optional).
initialValue: The value to start the accumulation. If not provided, the first element of the array is used as the initial accumulator.
On each iteration of callback function, the value returned from callback is assigned to accumulator

How the reduce() Internals Work:
To explain this in simpler terms, the reduce() method works like this behind the scenes:

Initialization: The reduce() method sets the initial value for the accumulator. If no initial value is provided,
it uses the first element of the array as the accumulator and starts the iteration from the second element.

Iteration: The callback function is invoked for each element in the array.
The accumulator keeps track of the cumulative result, and currentValue represents the current item in the array.

Update: After processing each element, the result is stored in the accumulator and passed to the next iteration.
This happens until all elements are processed.

Final Return: Once the iteration is complete, reduce() returns the final accumulated value.

const numbers = [1, 2, 3, 4];
const sum = numbers.reduce((accumulator, currentValue) => {
  return accumulator + currentValue;
}, 0);
console.log(sum);  // Output: 10
Step-by-Step Breakdown:
1. Initial Call to reduce()
The callback function is invoked for each element in the array.
The first argument to the reduce() function is the callback (accumulator, currentValue) => accumulator + currentValue,
and the second argument is the initial value 0.

2. First Iteration (index 0):
accumulator: 0 (the initialValue)

currentValue: 1 (the first element of the array)

result: 0 + 1 = 1 (this is the new value of the accumulator)

3. Second Iteration (index 1):
accumulator: 1 (from the result of the first iteration)

currentValue: 2 (the second element of the array)

result: 1 + 2 = 3

4. Third Iteration (index 2):
accumulator: 3 (from the result of the second iteration)

currentValue: 3 (the third element of the array)

result: 3 + 3 = 6

5. Fourth Iteration (index 3):
accumulator: 6 (from the result of the third iteration)

currentValue: 4 (the fourth element of the array)

result: 6 + 4 = 10

Final Result:
The final result after the last iteration is 10, and this value is returned as the output of reduce().

Use Cases:
1. Flattening an Array of Arrays
If you have a nested array and want to flatten it into a single value, reduce () can help

const nestedArrays = [[1, 2], [3, 4], [5, 6]];
const flattened = nestedArrays.reduce((accumulator, currentValue) => {
  return accumulator.concat(currentValue);
}, []);
console.log(flattened); // Output: [1, 2, 3, 4, 5, 6]

2. Sum of numbers
This is one of the most common uses for reduce. You can sum all the numbers in an array efficiently

const prices=[100,200,300]
const totalPrize=prices.reduce((total,price)=>total+price,0)
console.log(totalPrice);//output:600

3. Finding the Maximum or minimum value
reduce can also be used to find the largest or smallest number in an array

const numbers = [1, 5, 3, 9, 2];
const max = numbers.reduce((maxVal, currentVal) => (currentVal > maxVal ? currentVal : maxVal), numbers[0]);
console.log(max);  // Output: 9

4. Calculating the Average

const scores = [90, 85, 88, 92, 87];
const average = scores.reduce((accumulator, score, index, array) => {
  accumulator += score;
  if (index === array.length - 1) { // On the last iteration
    return accumulator / array.length;
  }
  return accumulator;
}, 0);
console.log(average); // Output: 88.4

5. Counting Occurrences of Items

const fruits = ['apple', 'banana', 'apple', 'orange', 'banana', 'apple'];
const fruitCount = fruits.reduce((accumulator, fruit) => {
  accumulator[fruit] = (accumulator[fruit] || 0) + 1;
  return accumulator;
}, {});
console.log(fruitCount);
// Output: { apple: 3, banana: 2, orange: 1 }

6. Building set of unique values, removing duplicates

const numbers = [1, 2, 2, 3, 4, 4, 5];
const uniqueNumbers = numbers.reduce((accumulator, currentValue) => {
  if (!accumulator.includes(currentValue)) {
    accumulator.push(currentValue);
  }
  return accumulator;
}, []);
console.log(uniqueNumbers); // Output: [1, 2, 3, 4, 5]

7. Grouping by property

const people = [
  { name: 'Alice', age: 25 },
  { name: 'Bob', age: 30 },
  { name: 'Charlie', age: 25 },
  { name: 'David', age: 30 },
  { name: 'Eve', age: 35 }
];
const groupedByAge = people.reduce((accumulator, person) => {
  const { age } = person;
  if (!accumulator[age]) {
    accumulator[age] = [];
  }
  accumulator[age].push(person);
  return accumulator;
}, {});
console.log(groupedByAge);
//output
{
  25: [
    { name: 'Alice', age: 25 },
    { name: 'Charlie', age: 25 }
  ],
  30: [
    { name: 'Bob', age: 30 },
    { name: 'David', age: 30 }
  ],
  35: [
    { name: 'Eve', age: 35 }
  ]
}

8. Transform Array to Object

You can convert an array of items into an object using reduce

const users=[
{id:1,name:'alice'},
{id:2, name:'bob'},
{id:3, name:'charlie'}
]

const userObj=users.reduce((acc,user)=>{
    acc[user.id]=user.name
    return acc;
},{})

console.log(userObj);//output {1:'alice',2:'bob',3:'charlie'}






